{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "import math\ndef poly(xs: list, x: float):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates polynomial with coefficients xs at point x.\n    return xs[0] + xs[1] * x + xs[1] * x^2 + .... xs[n] * x^n\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum([coeff * math.pow(x, i) for i, coeff in enumerate(xs)])\ndef _poly_derivative(xs: list, x: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the derivative of the polynomial at point x.\"\"\"\n    # Derivative of c_i * x^i is i * c_i * x^(i-1)\n    # The constant term (xs[0]) derivative is 0.\n    # So we start from i = 1.\n    return sum([i * coeff * math.pow(x, i - 1) for i, coeff in enumerate(xs) if i > 0])\ndef find_zero(xs: list) -> float:\n    \"\"\" xs are coefficients of a polynomial.\n    find_zero find x such that poly(x) = 0.\n    find_zero returns only only zero point, even if there are many.\n    Moreover, find_zero only takes list xs having even number of coefficients\n    and largest non zero coefficient as it guarantees\n    a solution.\n    >>> round(find_zero([1, 2]), 2) # f(x) = 1 + 2x\n    -0.5\n    >>> round(find_zero([-6, 11, -6, 1]), 2) # (x - 1) * (x - 2) * (x - 3) = -6 + 11x - 6x^2 + x^3\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    # Using Newton's method for finding a zero.\n    # Initial guess can be 0.0, as a solution is guaranteed for odd-degree polynomials.\n    x_current = 0.0\n    # Iterate a fixed number of times for convergence.\n    for _ in range(100):\n        poly_val = poly(xs, x_current)\n        deriv_val = _poly_derivative(xs, x_current)\n\n        # If derivative is zero, Newton's method fails. For this problem,\n        # assuming valid inputs mean this won't be a critical issue.\n        if deriv_val == 0:\n            break\n\n        x_next = x_current - poly_val / deriv_val\n        x_current = x_next\n    return x_current"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/32", "solution": "import math\ndef poly(xs: list, x: float):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates polynomial with coefficients xs at point x.\n    Returns xs[0] + xs[1]*x + ... + xs[n]*x^n.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sum([coeff * math.pow(x, i) for i, coeff in enumerate(xs)])\ndef find_zero(xs: list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds one approximate zero point (root) of the polynomial defined by coefficients xs using the bisection method.\n    It takes an even number of coefficients, guaranteeing a solution.\n    \"\"\"\n    TOLERANCE = 1e-6\n\n    # Initial check: if 0 is a root\n    if abs(poly(xs, 0.0)) <= TOLERANCE:\n        return 0.0\n\n    low = -1000.0\n    high = 1000.0\n\n    # Bisection loop\n    while (high - low) / 2.0 > TOLERANCE:\n        mid = (low + high) / 2.0\n        poly_at_mid_value = poly(xs, mid)\n\n        # Root check at midpoint\n        if abs(poly_at_mid_value) <= TOLERANCE:\n            return mid\n\n        # Interval narrowing\n        poly_at_low_value = poly(xs, low)\n        if poly_at_low_value * poly_at_mid_value < 0:\n            high = mid\n        else:\n            low = mid\n\n    # Return midpoint of the final interval if loop terminates by width\n    return (low + high) / 2.0"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "def sort_third(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    \"\"\"\n    output_list = list(l)\n    divisible_by_three_elements = []\n    divisible_by_three_indices = []\n\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            divisible_by_three_elements.append(l[i])\n            divisible_by_three_indices.append(i)\n\n    divisible_by_three_elements.sort()\n\n    for j in range(len(divisible_by_three_indices)):\n        original_index = divisible_by_three_indices[j]\n        output_list[original_index] = divisible_by_three_elements[j]\n\n    return output_list"}
